{"name":"Blue Romance","hex":"#d8f0d2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"romance","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An intimate relationship between two people; a love affair.","A strong obsession or attachment for something or someone."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To woo; to court.","To write or tell romantic stories, poetry, letters, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/romance"}],"entries":[{"title":"Romance","blocks":[{"text":"Romance and romantic love encompass a number of ideas about love, which are interrelated for historical and cultural reasons:"},{"items":["passionate feelings of attraction—a mental state of \"being in love\", with focused attention (salience) towards a specific individual for courtship or pair bonding;","the cultural practice or idealization of initiating intimate relationships for feelings like these, over more practical or ordinary concerns;","a relationship or love affair initiated or maintained this way, which may be premarital or absent a commitment; and","a love story involving these elements."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Frank_Bernard_Dicksee_-_Romeo_and_Juliet%2C_1884.jpg/330px-Frank_Bernard_Dicksee_-_Romeo_and_Juliet%2C_1884.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romance"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png/500px-Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png/500px-Mesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dopamine is produced in the ventral tegmental area (VTA) of the brain, and projected to the nucleus accumbens (NAc). Dopamine activity in the NAc is key to the attribution of salience.[84]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesocorticolimbic_Circuit.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/500px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/1280px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg/500px-Colour_Wheel_of_Love.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p